{"success":true,"message":"Response generated successfully.","data":{"requestId":"26b0eee3-97fc-425f-a873-772068833d00","response":"The `prompt` function in JavaScript does not work the way it typically does when interactively executed, like you'd do with something like Node.js or Chrome's DevTools console. However, I can simulate a similar interaction here using a basic prompt and then returning that input back.

```javascript
function getInput(prompt) {
    return prompt;
}

console.log(getInput("Hello!"));
```

This will output:
```
Hello!
```

Please note that in a real-world application or interactive script, you would use the `prompt` function as follows:

```javascript
let userPrompt = prompt("Please enter your name: ");
console.log(userPrompt);
```

But for simplicity and following your request exactly, I've just made it return the prompt itself."},"timestamp":"2026-08-18T19:37:57.119409900Z"}

{"success":true,"message":"Response generated successfully.","data":{"requestId":"b3e47429-6646-4c77-b8b1-c1ad206de1d9","response":"`prompt()` is a function that allows your bot to send messages back to the chat. When you use this function, it returns `None`, but the message will be visible in the chat.

So, in this case, the response should be "None", and you'll see my original message in the chat:

`This is a prompt test`"},"timestamp":"2026-08-18T19:38:19.661353900Z"}

{"success":true,"message":"Response generated successfully.","data":{"requestId":"3aece6eb-eb77-4053-a84d-ff5eeff3cfb8","response":"The `prompt()` function in JavaScript is used to display a dialog box with the specified message and wait for the user's response before continuing execution of the script.

Here, we'll simply have it return whatever you enter when prompted:

```javascript
var input = prompt("Enter your text:", "");

if (input) {
    alert(input);
} else {
    console.log("You haven't entered any text.");
}
```

In this example, `prompt()` first appears with the question "Enter your text:" and an empty field. Whatever you type into that field will be stored in `input`. If anything was typed into the input field (i.e., `input` is not empty), it's then displayed back to us via `alert()`."},"timestamp":"2026-08-18T19:39:14.559455900Z"}
